## Proof

**Setup.** Let $S^n \subset \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ with coordinates $(x_0,\dots,x_n)$. The equator is $S^{n-1} = S^n \cap \{x_n = 0\}$. The upper hemisphere $D^n_+ = \{x_n \ge 0\} \cap S^n$ and lower hemisphere $D^n_- = \{x_n \le 0\} \cap S^n$ are both closed $n$-balls with common boundary $S^{n-1}$.

The quotient map $q: S^n \to S^n \vee S^n$ collapses the equator $S^{n-1}$ to a single point $*$. Since $D^n_+/\partial D^n_+ \cong S^n$ and $D^n_-/\partial D^n_- \cong S^n$, and these two copies share the point $*$, the quotient is indeed $S^n \vee S^n$. Denote the two summand spheres by $S^n_1$ (from $D^n_+$) and $S^n_2$ (from $D^n_-$).

**Goal.** Compute $q_*: H_n(S^n) \to H_n(S^n \vee S^n) \cong \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$.

**Decomposition of the fundamental class.** Give $S^n$ its standard orientation. Both $D^n_+$ and $D^n_-$ inherit orientations from $S^n$. The induced orientation on $\partial D^n_+$ (equator with outward normal of $D^n_+$, which points in the $-x_n$ direction) is opposite to the induced orientation on $\partial D^n_-$ (outward normal points in the $+x_n$ direction). Therefore the boundaries cancel:

$$\partial[D^n_+, \partial D^n_+] + \partial[D^n_-, \partial D^n_-] = 0,$$

so $[D^n_+, \partial D^n_+] + [D^n_-, \partial D^n_-]$ is a cycle representing $[S^n] \in H_n(S^n)$.

**Action of $q$ on each piece.** The restriction $q|_{D^n_+}: D^n_+ \to S^n_1$ is the quotient map $D^n_+ \to D^n_+/\partial D^n_+ \cong S^n_1$, which is a homeomorphism on the interior and sends the boundary to the basepoint. This map sends the relative fundamental class $[D^n_+, \partial D^n_+]$ to $[S^n_1]$, i.e., it has degree $+1$.

Likewise, $q|_{D^n_-}: D^n_- \to S^n_2$ is the quotient $D^n_- \to D^n_-/\partial D^n_- \cong S^n_2$, sending $[D^n_-, \partial D^n_-]$ to $[S^n_2]$ with degree $+1$.

**Computation.** Let $\alpha = [S^n]$ be the generator of $H_n(S^n) \cong \mathbb{Z}$. Then:

$$q_*(\alpha) = q_*\big([D^n_+, \partial D^n_+] + [D^n_-, \partial D^n_-]\big) = [S^n_1] + [S^n_2].$$

Under the canonical isomorphism $H_n(S^n \vee S^n) \cong H_n(S^n_1) \oplus H_n(S^n_2) \cong \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$, where the first component is the first sphere and the second component is the second sphere, this gives:

$$\boxed{q_*(1) = (1,\, 1), \qquad \text{i.e., } q_*(k) = (k,\, k).}$$

**Verification via projections.** Let $p_i: S^n \vee S^n \to S^n$ ($i=1,2$) collapse the other sphere to the basepoint. Then $p_1 \circ q: S^n \to S^n$ collapses the closed lower hemisphere $D^n_-$ to a point, i.e., it is the quotient $S^n \to S^n/D^n_- \cong S^n$. This quotient is a homeomorphism on $\operatorname{int}(D^n_+)$ and preserves orientation, so $\deg(p_1 \circ q) = 1$. By the same argument, $\deg(p_2 \circ q) = 1$. Since $(p_i)_*$ recovers the $i$-th component of $q_*$, this confirms $q_*(1) = (1,1)$.

**Remark.** This map $q_*: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$, $1 \mapsto (1,1)$, is the **comultiplication** (coproduct) on $H_n(S^n)$, which is dual to the cup product multiplication $H^n(S^n) \oplus H^n(S^n) \to H^n(S^n)$ in cohomology.

